use ../../lib/CodingRule

from ../CppCoreGuidelines import CppCoreGuidelines

export coding-rule CppCorePer7:
    standard: @{CppCoreGuidelines}
    ruleId: Per.7
    title: Shape designs so they can be optimized later
    statement:
        Design interfaces that leave room for a faster implementation later.
        Give them enough information, such as the element type, the size and
        the comparison to use. Prefer compact data, pass arguments in the
        usual ways, and hide awkward legacy code behind an interface you
        control.
    rationale:
        Initial designs almost always need performance work at some point.
        An interface that discards information, like one working on untyped
        memory, is hard to evolve and denies the compiler what it needs to
        optimize.
    exceptions:
        Code that is purely a one-off implementation detail does not need
        this kind of forward-looking interface.
    severity: null
    noncompliant:
        ```cpp
        \\\
        // Element type and size are lost; caller must supply a byte-level comparator.
        void sort_records(void* base, std::size_t count, std::size_t size,
                          int (*compare)(const void*, const void*));
        \\\
        ```
    compliant:
        ```cpp
        \\\
        template<std::random_access_iterator It, class Compare = std::less<>>
        void sort_records(It first, It last, Compare compare = {});
        \\\
        ```
    languages: [cpp]
    tags: [performance, interfaces, design]
    source: https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#rper-efficiency
